title The hardest solvable Sound crux is the first Glow core -- several gates, one source, one lowered module
prompt Keaton re-armed Mind and asked for Sound's hardest solvable Lindy-first crux-first problem.
think Read the lane against both priorities. Lindy: the module form is a language's spine -- the ancestor's core rune has carried its userspace for over a decade, and ours would carry every interface surface Grain grows. Crux: the boundary between a language that mirrors one law per desk and a language that authors modules is the single door everything in the interface lane queues behind.
think The growth law's test passes today: the Lantern face's three laws speak Glow individually in three GREEN desks yet cannot share one home; the Rye that holds them as one module is pond/apps/lantern_face.rye; the desk that would speak it is gate-lantern-face.glow -- one source, three gates, one lowered Rye module with the wall constant.
think Solvable now because the parity oracle already stands: the lantern face witness runs GREEN against the hand-written module, so the lowered module must pass the same unchanged witness. The lower needs one structural growth -- a source holding N gate specs -- and no new operator or condition shape.
obs Runners-up ranked and named: the value model's cords follow the core (new kinds need a home), gate-calls-gate follows the core, LOCA's next rungs sit partly behind custody gates, and %374 is Keaton's by name.
